[2026-09-17] rc:4042200929 inline on src/validation.cpp by ajtowns (member):
Types guarantee at compile time that no code path will violate the invariant; a unit test can only verify the code paths that the unit test exercises don't violate the invariant, and `GetBlockScriptFlags` is heavily reliant on chain state, so exactly the sort of function where unit tests might miss cases. This was added to address [this earlier review comment](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/29843#discussion_r3052418404).

I'm open to making it easier to understand; for me "I want to guarantee that `x` only takes values from `Y`" is something you express by declaring `x` to be of type `Y`, so, to me, it already seems like it says what it means.  I don't believe there's a way to exhaustively extract "these are all the values this function can output" in C++; you can only do constraints like that thoroughly via the type system.
